Illusionism  [  [ edit
](/w/index.php?title=Eliminative_materialism&action=edit&section=21 "Edit
section: Illusionism") ]

**Illusionism** is an active program within eliminative materialism to explain
[ phenomenal consciousness ](/wiki/Phenomenal_consciousness "Phenomenal
consciousness") as an illusion. It is promoted by the philosophers [ Daniel
Dennett ](/wiki/Daniel_Dennett "Daniel Dennett") , [ Keith Frankish
](/wiki/Keith_Frankish "Keith Frankish") , and [ Jay Garfield
](/wiki/Jay_L._Garfield "Jay L. Garfield") , and the neuroscientist [ Michael
Graziano ](/wiki/Michael_Graziano "Michael Graziano") .  [64]  [65]  Graziano
has advanced the [ attention schema theory of consciousness
](/wiki/Attention_schema_theory "Attention schema theory") and postulates that
consciousness is an illusion.  [66]  [67]  According to [ David Chalmers
](/wiki/David_Chalmers "David Chalmers") , proponents argue that once we can
explain consciousness as an illusion without the need for a realist view of
consciousness, we can construct a debunking argument against realist views of
consciousness.  [68]  This line of argument draws from other debunking
arguments like the [ evolutionary debunking argument
](/wiki/Evolutionary_debunking "Evolutionary debunking") in the field of [
metaethics ](/wiki/Meta-ethics "Meta-ethics") . Such arguments note that
morality is explained by evolution without positing [ moral realism
](/wiki/Moral_realism "Moral realism") , so there is a sufficient basis to
debunk moral realism.  [69]

